infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Real timer vs fake timer: the same test, 5000 ms to microseconds
  desc Faking the clock removes real time, so the slowest, flakiest test becomes the fastest and deterministic
  compares
    - label Real timer (wall clock)
      children
        - label Waits the full 5000 ms
          desc A 5s setTimeout backoff really takes 5 seconds every run
        - label Flakes ~1 run in 20
          desc Under CI load the timer fires late and the assertion races ahead
        - label Coupled to the wall clock
          desc Non-deterministic — the test depends on real elapsed time
        - label Gets marked skip
          desc Muted instead of fixed, so a real backoff regression slips through
    - label Fake timer (virtual clock)
      children
        - label Runs in microseconds
          desc mock.timers.tick(5000) advances virtual time instantly, no real wait
        - label 100% deterministic
          desc No real time means no race between the timer and the assertion
        - label Controllable clock
          desc mock.timers.enable swaps setTimeout and Date for a virtual one
        - label Four-line fix
          desc Fake, advance, assert — no production code touched
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
